export const RUBRIC_ANCHORS = "## 4. Anchors: what a 2, a 5, and an 8 look like\n\nAnchors are written for **seed-stage health-product onboarding flows** (signup → intake → first meaningful action). Rules of use:\n\n- Rate against the anchor language, citing the checklist criteria observed. Scores between anchors are interpolated by judgment (a 6 or 7 means \"clearly better than the 5 anchor, not yet the 8 anchor\").\n- 0–1 is reserved for flows that are broken or absent; 9–10 for flows with no meaningful violations and evident deliberate craft.\n- The Critical-cap (Section 1.4) overrides any anchor: a Critical finding caps the dimension at 4.\n- Research citations name the vault file the pattern or stat comes from; criterion IDs come from the checklist only.\n\n### ONB — Onboarding & Activation\n\n- **2** — Onboarding is a forced, front-loaded gauntlet: a modal walkthrough or deck-of-cards tutorial precedes any real use (ONB-01), configuration and preference-setting are demanded before the user has seen value (ONB-07), and post-signup screens are blank with no path to a first action (ONB-06). Health context makes this worse: sensitive data is requested before trust is established — the August AI case observed 42% abandonment when sensitive data was requested too early (case-studies/00-master-index.md).\n- **5** — A new user can reach a first meaningful action, but not within ~30 seconds and not without reading instructions (ONB-03). Help exists but is push-style and, once dismissed, cannot be recalled (ONB-01, ONB-04). Some empty states guide; others are blank (ONB-06). Complexity is partially staged but at least one advanced-feature detour interrupts the path to first value (ONB-02).\n- **8** — Simplest-path-to-first-value is evident: complexity is progressively disclosed (ONB-02), first meaningful action lands inside ~30 seconds (ONB-03), help is contextual pull-style, dismissible, and re-findable (ONB-01, ONB-04), and setup is deferred until after value (ONB-07). Matches the pattern behind Firefly Health's branching onboarding, which explained the care model before collecting data (case-studies/00-master-index.md).\n\n### FORM — Forms & Input\n\n- **2** — Intake/signup forms ask for non-essential fields upfront (FORM-01), rely on placeholder-only labels (FORM-05), reveal formatting rules only on error (FORM-08), and errors are vague, color-only, or wipe user input (FORM-10). In a health intake, this is the profile CHI 2014 associates with 42% first-try success (09-forms-design.md).\n- **5** — Field count is mostly justified and labels are persistent (FORM-01, FORM-05), but requirements are discovered by failed submission (FORM-07, FORM-08) and error messages identify the field without saying how to fix it (FORM-10). Layout or sequencing quirks (multi-column stretches, unexpected field order — FORM-03, FORM-04) slow completion.\n- **8** — Every field is essential or deferred (FORM-01), single-column with logical sequencing (FORM-03, FORM-04), required/optional and format rules visible before typing (FORM-07, FORM-08), and errors are specific, adjacent, multi-cue, and input-preserving (FORM-10). Comparable to the LYKON merged login/signup with real-time validation pattern (case-studies/00-master-index.md).\n\n### N — Nielsen's 10 Heuristics\n\n- **2** — The flow routinely leaves users guessing: no feedback on submissions or loading (N-01), no way to go back or exit steps without losing work (N-03), inconsistent terms and controls between steps (N-04), and error states that neither explain nor help (N-09).\n- **5** — Core actions give feedback and back-navigation mostly works (N-01, N-03), but consistency slips between signup and in-product screens (N-04), some medical or internal jargon leaks into labels (N-02), and at least one error state is unhelpful (N-09).\n- **8** — Status is always visible, escape routes are clearly marked, language matches how patients/users talk about their problem, and errors are plain-language and recoverable (N-01, N-02, N-03, N-09). Remaining violations are isolated and Minor.\n\n### CRO — Conversion entry (signup surface)\n\n- **2** — A first-time visitor can't tell what the product does, who it's for, or what to do next within 5 seconds (CRO-01); the primary conversion action buries intent under extra steps or forced account creation (CRO-08); nothing communicates what happens after the click (CRO-10).\n- **5** — Value proposition and primary CTA are present but generic (CRO-02, CRO-05); the conversion path works but carries removable friction — extra steps or fields between intent and action (CRO-08); post-signup expectations are only partially set (CRO-10).\n- **8** — Above-the-fold clarity passes the 5-second test (CRO-01), CTAs are specific and placed at decision points (CRO-02), friction between intent and action is minimal (CRO-08), and the post-CTA state tells users exactly what happens next (CRO-10). Baymard's guidance on explicit next-step labeling applies (ux-case-studies/05-calls-to-action.md).\n\n### IXD — Interaction Design\n\n- **2** — Taps and clicks frequently produce no visible response within ~100ms and long operations show no loading state (IXD-01); key targets are small or ambiguous (IXD-02, IXD-03); single steps overload the user with choices (IXD-04).\n- **5** — Feedback exists for most actions but at least one operation over 1 second runs silent (IXD-01); interactive elements are mostly identifiable but some clickable items don't look clickable (IXD-03); the primary task completes, but with avoidable steps or modals (IXD-05).\n- **8** — Every interaction acknowledges input immediately, targets are comfortably sized, signifiers are consistent, choices are chunked, and the path to first value is near the minimum interaction cost (IXD-01, IXD-02, IXD-03, IXD-05).\n\n### COPY — Content & Copywriting\n\n- **2** — Clinical or product-internal jargon dominates (COPY-07); the flow fails the 5-second comprehension test (COPY-01); walls of unscannable text (COPY-06); error and confirmation microcopy is absent or unhelpful (COPY-12).\n- **5** — Mostly plain language with jargon lapses (COPY-07); headings support scanning but key screens bury the point (COPY-06, COPY-13); tone wobbles between clinical and casual across steps (COPY-10); error copy exists but doesn't always say what to do next (COPY-12).\n- **8** — Plain, benefit-led, scannable copy throughout (COPY-02, COPY-06, COPY-07); consistent, context-appropriate tone; error and empty-state messaging tells users what happened and what to do (COPY-12). Mirrors the Korea University Medical Center pattern of replacing medical jargon with plain language in patient intake (case-studies/00-master-index.md).\n\n### ACC — Accessibility (WCAG 2.1 AA)\n\n- **2** — Widespread objective violations: text below 4.5:1 contrast (ACC-06), form inputs without programmatic labels (ACC-02), keyboard traps or unreachable controls (ACC-13, ACC-14), color as the only error indicator (ACC-05).\n- **5** — Automated scans are mostly clean on contrast and alt text (ACC-01, ACC-06), but form errors aren't programmatically associated with fields (ACC-25), focus indicators are weak or missing in places (ACC-21), and at least one flow step is awkward or impossible by keyboard (ACC-13).\n- **8** — No Critical automated violations; labels, error identification, and instructions meet ACC-02, ACC-25, ACC-26; keyboard and focus behavior is deliberate (ACC-13, ACC-21). Note the confidence caveat: automated tools catch ~30–40% of WCAG violations (checklist Sec. 5), so an 8 on the Preview tier reflects automated evidence only.\n\n### MOB — Mobile UX\n\n- **2** — Touch targets under ~1cm and mis-tap-prone spacing (MOB-01); text entry is heavy with mismatched keyboards (MOB-05); overlays are accidentally dismissible and lose input (MOB-08); content is desktop-shrunk, forcing horizontal scroll (MOB-04).\n- **5** — Targets and keyboards are mostly right (MOB-01, MOB-05), but primary actions sit outside comfortable thumb reach (MOB-02) and some content isn't rewritten for mobile (MOB-04). Typing hasn't been minimized with autofill or device capabilities (MOB-05).\n- **8** — Comfortable targets and spacing, thumb-reachable primary actions, minimized typing with correct keyboards, and mobile-composed content (MOB-01, MOB-02, MOB-04, MOB-05). This is the bar the 84%-smartphone-first health context demands (case-studies/00-master-index.md).\n\n### VD — Visual & Brand Design\n\n- **2** — No clear visual hierarchy — primary actions don't stand out (VD-02); inconsistent type and color use step-to-step (VD-03, VD-04); the design signals neither warmth nor clinical credibility for a health buyer (VD-01, VD-08).\n- **5** — Hierarchy is present but primary and secondary actions compete on some screens (VD-02); the palette and type scale are mostly consistent with lapses (VD-03, VD-04); trust signaling is generic rather than tuned to health expectations (VD-08).\n- **8** — Clear first-read hierarchy on every screen, consistent system usage, interactive states distinct (VD-02, VD-04, VD-09), and trust signaling calibrated to health-and-wellness conventions — warmth balanced with clinical credibility (VD-08; industry-ux-expectations.md).\n\n### IA — Information Architecture (in-flow)\n\n- **2** — Users can't tell where they are in the flow or how much remains (IA-03); labels use internal terminology (IA-01); dead ends with no path forward (IA-06); weak information scent forces backtracking (IA-10).\n- **5** — Steps are labeled and mostly predictable (IA-01, IA-03), but progress orientation is inconsistent and one or two labels don't predict what's behind them (IA-10).\n- **8** — Users always know where they are, what's next, and how to get back (IA-02, IA-03); labels are plain and scent is strong throughout (IA-01, IA-10).\n\n### QA — Bugs & Defects (dynamic — no checklist IDs)\n\nThe checklist classifies QA as a dynamic dimension assessed live, with no criterion table; anchors therefore reference observed defect behavior, not IDs.\n\n- **2** — Reproducible functional defects on the activation path: failed submissions, broken links or buttons, state loss on back-navigation, console errors with visible impact.\n- **5** — The path completes, but with cosmetic defects, intermittent glitches, or non-blocking errors that erode confidence (the NHS App case documents this trust-erosion pattern; case-studies/00-master-index.md).\n- **8** — No observed defects on the activation path across the evaluated devices/sessions; edge interactions (refresh mid-flow, back button) behave sanely.\n\n### PERF — Performance & Core Web Vitals (Preview Score URL profile ONLY)\n\n- **2** — Lighthouse performance score below 50 on mobile (PERF-01); LCP over 4.0s and/or CLS over 0.25 — \"Poor\" on Google's thresholds (PERF-02, PERF-03).\n- **5** — Scores in the 50–89 \"Needs Improvement\" range (PERF-01); one or more Core Web Vitals in the middle band, e.g., LCP 2.5–4.0s (PERF-02); identifiable causes such as unoptimized images or render-blocking resources (PERF-05, PERF-06).\n- **8** — Lighthouse 90+ on mobile and desktop (PERF-01); LCP ≤ 2.5s, CLS ≤ 0.1, INP ≤ 200ms (PERF-02, PERF-03, PERF-04). If PageSpeed data is unavailable, PERF is marked \"Cannot Evaluate\" and omitted from the profile (checklist Sec. 6), not guessed.";
